Looking at the type declarations, I need to understand how groups reference their animated properties. The key structures are:

- `AnimationGroup.targetedAnimations` — static declarations of target + path
- `AnimationGroup._propertyMixer` — runtime tracks (when blending is active), each with `mixTarget()` and `mixProperty`

The strategy: for each group that animates `target` + `path`, remove the matching tracks from its mixer. If no tracks remain, stop the group. For groups without a mixer (direct-write mode), stop the group entirely since there's no way to selectively disable a single track.

FILE: app/src/release.ts
```ts
import { getAnimationGroups, stopAnimation } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import type { AnimationManager } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function releaseProperty(manager: AnimationManager, target: object, path: string): void {
    const groups = getAnimationGroups(manager);

    for (const group of groups) {
        // Determine whether this group animates the given (target, path).
        let matches = false;

        // Check the static targeted-animation declarations.
        for (const ta of group.targetedAnimations) {
            if (ta.target === target && ta.path === path) {
                matches = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Also check the runtime mixer tracks (covers cases where
        // targetedAnimations is absent or stale).
        if (!matches && group._propertyMixer) {
            const tracks = group._propertyMixer[0];
            for (const track of tracks) {
                if (track.mixTarget() === target && track.mixProperty === path) {
                    matches = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        if (!matches) continue;

        // This group writes to our target+path.  Remove those tracks.
        if (group._propertyMixer) {
            const [tracks, a, b, c, d] = group._propertyMixer;
            const remaining = tracks.filter(
                (t) => !(t.mixTarget() === target && t.mixProperty === path),
            );
            if (remaining.length === 0) {
                stopAnimation(group);
            } else {
                group._propertyMixer = [remaining, a, b, c, d];
            }
        } else {
            // No mixer (direct-write mode): the only way to stop the
            // group from writing that property is to stop the group.
            stopAnimation(group);
        }
    }
}
```

I wrote `src/release.ts` which iterates every group on the manager, identifies groups that animate the given target+path (checking both `targetedAnimations` and `_propertyMixer` tracks), and either filters the offending tracks out of the group's mixer (stopping the group if none remain) or stops the group outright when no mixer exists. The one thing I was not sure of: for a non-blended (direct-write) group that animates *multiple* properties of the same target, stopping the whole group is the only option available through this API — there is no way to suppress a single track without a mixer — so such a group will be fully stopped even though it also animates other properties of that target.
